S'Mary Beth, a museum tour guide, takes a group of school detention students on a secret museum tour, telling them, with wooden figures, the story of a Mexican town called San Angel from the Book of Life, holding every story in the world. On the Day of the Dead, La Muerte, ruler of the Land of the Remembered, and Xibalba, ruler of the Land of the Forgotten, set a wager for their own worlds after seeing Manolo S\xc3\xa1nchez and Joaqu\xc3\xadn Mondragon competing over Mar\xc3\xada Posada. La Muerte bets on Manolo while Xibalba bets on Joaqu\xc3\xadn. However, Xibalba cheats by giving Joaquin his Medal of Everlasting Life, granting the wearer invincibility. Mar\xc3\xada frees a herd of captive pigs, resulting her being sent to a boarding school in Spain. Parting ways, Manolo gives her one of the rescued baby pigs, which he named Chuy, and she gives him a guitar engraved with the phrase "Always play from the heart". Years later, Manolo\'s dreams are suppressed by his father Carlos, who trains him to be a bullfighter to honor their family, while Joaqu\xc3\xadn becomes a hero with the Medal\'s aid. On Manolo\'s first bullfight, Mar\xc3\xada returns. Manolo defeats the bull but refuses to kill it, dismaying Carlos and the crowd but impressing Mar\xc3\xada. That night, Mar\xc3\xada is pressured by her father and mayor, General Ramiro Posada, to marry Joaqu\xc3\xadn for his protection from the Bandit King Chakal. Mar\xc3\xada and Manolo both profess their love before dawn, but are interrupted when Xibalba sends his dual-headed snake staff to bite her once to send her into a coma state. Devastated by Mar\xc3\xada\'s "death", Manolo allows the snake to bite him twice, tricking him into thinking he would be reunited with Mar\xc3\xada in death. At the Land of the Remembered, Manolo reunites with his mother Carmen and his ancestral family. They travel to La Muerte\'s castle, discovering Xibalba, now the new ruler of the Land of the Remembered, who explains the bet and that the snake killed him, not Mar\xc3\xada. Mar\xc3\xada awakens (thanks of Joaquin\'s Medal), she learns of Manolo\'s death and accepts Joaqu\xc3\xadn\'s proposal. Manolo, Carmen, and his grandfather Luis travel to the Cave of Souls to reach La Muerte. Inside, they meet the Candle Maker, the overseer of mortal lives and the Book of Life\'s keeper. After seeing that Manolo\'s story in the Book of Life is blank and can be rewritten by himself, he takes them to the Land of the Forgotten. They finds La Muerte at Xibalba\'s previous castle and exposes the cheat. Furious, La Muerte lectures Xibalba before offering a new wager: Manolo\'s life will be returned if he completes a challenge of Xibalba\'s choosing, but if he fails, he will be forgotten and Xibalba will keep both realms. Xibalba sets Manolo against a giant bull skeleton (made out of other bull skeletons that his family killed through centuries). In the Land of the Living, Chakal leads his army to San Angel (in middle of Mar\xc3\xada and Joaquin\'s wedding) to find the Medal, which was previously his. Chakal kills Carlos, arrives in time to see Manolo\'s fight. Manolo, realizing his fear is actually being himself, refuses fighting and sings an apology, which peacefully dissolves the bull and also touches his family, even the deities (especially Xilbalba). Impressed, the deities restore Manolo to life and send him and his family to San Angel to protect it from Chakal and his army. Manolo is seemingly killed when Chakal blows himself up, but he is protected by the Medal, which Joaqu\xc3\xadn had slipped him earlier. Joaqu\xc3\xadn returns it to Xibalba and resolves to be a true hero, while Manolo and Mar\xc3\xada are happily married as Xibalba and La Muerte reconcile. Back at the present: Mary Beth finishes Manolo, Mar\xc3\xada and Joaquin\'s story, the now-amazed children leave the museum. Mary Beth and an elderly security guard named Guicho reveal themselves to be La Muerte and Xibalba in disguises (who are still reconciled at one another). Then the Candle Maker appears and encourages the audience by showing them the Book of Life in front of them and tells them to write their own story.'
