S"Dr. Harry Dalton (Pierce Brosnan), a volcanologist of the United States Geological Survey, and his partner, Marianne (Walker Brandt), are studying volcanic activity in Colombia when the volcano erupts. As they try to escape, Marianne is killed by a volcanic bomb. Harry mourns her death, believing it could have been prevented by evacuating sooner. Four years later, Harry is assigned by his boss Dr. Paul Dreyfus (Charles Hallahan) to investigate seismic activity at Dante's Peak, Washington, a small town situated near a dormant stratovolcano in the Cascades. Harry arrives as the town is celebrating its founding, and meets the town's mayor, Rachel Wando (Linda Hamilton), and her two children Graham (Jeremy Foley) and Lauren (Jamie Ren\xc3\xa9e Smith). Rachel offers to take Harry up the volcano while visiting her former mother in-law Ruth (Elizabeth Hoffman). There, they find dead wildlife and two dead bathers in the hot springs, boiled alive. Believing the excessive heat is a result of volcanic activity, Harry asks Paul to bring a team to study the mountain further. The team arrives with additional equipment, but they do not corroborate Harry's fears and suggest there is little concern. Regardless, Harry tries to convince Rachel to prepare the town for a possible disaster, while falling into a budding relationship with her. A week passes without any signs of volcanic activity and Paul orders the team to pack up. On his last evening with Rachel, Harry discovers the town's water supply to be contaminated with volcanic residue, which convinces his team of an impending eruption. While preparations are made to evacuate, the volcano erupts, sending the townspeople into a panic. As Paul and the team contact the National Guard for help, Harry and Rachel go after the children, who have gone to get Ruth. Harry and Rachel reach Ruth's cabin just as a lava flow engulfs it, forcing them to abandon their vehicles and cross a nearby lake via a motorboat. Close to the shore of the lake, the boat's engine fails, and they realize the lake's waters have become acidic as a result of sulfur-rich gases dissolving in the water, with the resultant sulfuric acid eating away at the boat. Ruth willingly gets out of the boat to push it to shore, leaving her with severe chemical burns to which she later succumbs. The next morning, the National Guard arrives and the town is evacuated. On the mountain glaciers and snow melt. The flood into the river, picking up boulders and trees, now becoming a lahar, it overflows a nearby dam and causes it to collapse. The flood the heads towards the town and sweeps away the bridge leading out of it. The team makes it across, but Paul is washed away in the flood. Meanwhile, Harry and the Wandos take a pickup truck from a ranger station and drive back down the mountain. A lava flow blocks their path, but they eventually manage to get across it, rescuing Ruth's dog, Roughy, along the way. Back in Dante's Peak, Harry retrieves a distress radiobeacon from the motel his team used as a base, and finds out on a computer that the mountain is due for a final catastrophic eruption. With no time to escape, he drives into the town's abandoned mine just as the mountain violently and laterally explodes. The giant ash cloud burst out of the volcano and quickly condences into a Pyroclastic flow and flows down the mountain destroying everything in its path. Tree's, houses, and the town itself. The flow bears down on the town, obliterating everything in its path; Harty manages to drive the truck strait into the mine as the cloud passes over them. Elsewhere, Harry's team presumes him dead as they watch the eruption from afar. In the mine, Harry realizes he left the beacon inside the truck and goes to retrieve it. The mine collapses and he is injured, but he manages to enter the truck and activate the beacon. Days later, after the eruption has ended, Harry's team notices the signal from the beacon and dispatches rescue crews. Harry and the Wandos are freed and flown out by helicopter. As the credits roll, the camera pans out over the destroyed town before turning to the volcano, now reduced to a menacing caldera resembling Mount St. Helens; ominous background music hints it will erupt again in the future."
