S'In Prague, James Bond assassinates turncoat MI6 section chief Dryden in his office as well as his terrorist contact in a bathroom in Lahore, earning him a licence to kill and status as a 00 agent. In Uganda, the mysterious liaison Mr. White introduces warlord Steven Obanno to Le Chiffre, a terrorist financier. Obanno entrusts Chiffre with a large sum of money, which Chiffre uses to short-sell stock in the aerospace company Skyfleet, thus betting the money on the company\'s failure. In Madagascar, Bond pursues bomb maker Mollaka to an African embassy, shooting him dead and blowing up the building. In London, MI6 chief M admonishes Bond for his rashness, and advises him to rethink his future as an agent. Clues from Mollaka point to corrupt Greek official Alex Dimitrios. Bond finds Dimitrios in the Bahamas and after seducing his wife Solange, pursues him to Miami. Bond kills Dimitrios and follows his henchman to the airport. He thwarts the destruction of Skyfleet\'s airliner, costing Chiffre his investment. To recoup the money, Chiffre sets up a high-stakes Texas hold \'em tournament at the Casino Royale in Montenegro. MI6 enters Bond in the tournament, believing a defeat will force Chiffre to aid the British government in exchange for protection from his creditors. On the train to Montenegro, Bond meets Vesper Lynd, a British Treasury agent there to protect the government\'s $10 million buy-in. In Montenegro, Bond and Vesper meet their MI6 contact, Ren\xc3\xa9 Mathis. Bond gains the upper hand at the start of the game until a break, where Obanno, infuriated over the loss of his money, ambushes Chiffre in his suite. Bond engages Obanno, and strangles him to death in an ensuing struggle. Vesper is traumatised, but Bond holds her comfortingly. When the tournament resumes, Bond loses his initial stake, and Vesper refuses to fund further playing. Frustrated, Bond goes to assassinate Chiffre when he meets Felix Leiter, fellow player and CIA agent on the same mission as Bond. Leiter, who is on the verge of losing, agrees to stake Bond in exchange for custody of Chiffre. Bond rapidly rebuilds his position before another break occurs. Chiffre\'s girlfriend Valenka spikes Bond\'s martini with poison. Bond throws up and retreats to his Aston Martin to inject an antidote. MI6 instructs him to use the defibrillator, but he finds one of the wires disconnected. Vesper saves Bond by reconnecting the wires and pushing the button. Bond returns to the game just as Leiter loses his last hand to Chiffre. The tournament then culminates in a 150-million hand where the remaining players, including Bond and Chiffre, unanimously go all in. Chiffre trumps the other players, but Bond defeats him and wins the game with a straight flush. Chiffre captures Vesper and Bond, revealing Mathis as his mole in MI6. Chiffre tortures Bond for the password to the account containing his money, but Bond refuses to give in. As Chiffre prepares to castrate Bond, White bursts in and shoots him dead. Bond awakens in a hospital on Lake Como as Mathis is taken in by MI6. Bond and Vesper fall in love, and he decides to resign from MI6 to be with her. Bond and Vesper travel to Venice. M calls Bond and tells him the money was never deposited. Realising Vesper has stolen it, Bond pursues her and her clients into a building under renovation. The building is damaged in the struggle and begins to sink into the Grand Canal. Bond kills Vesper\'s accomplices and tries to rescue her, but she dies by drowning. White, watching nearby, walks away with the money. Bond rejoins MI6, and copes with Vesper\'s death by renouncing her as a traitor. M informs him the same organisation behind Chiffre had kidnapped Vesper\'s lover, and threatened to kill him unless she became a double agent. During the torture sessions with Chiffre, Vesper had made a deal: the money in exchange for Bond\'s life. Bond discovers a text message left for him by Vesper with White\'s name and phone number. At his estate in Lake Como, White receives a phone call. Asking for the other person\'s identity, he is shot in the leg as Bond strolls into his path holding a machine gun. Bond answers the question: "The name\'s Bond... James Bond."'
